  "content": {
    "id": "p13-q13-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "13",
    "prompt": "Suppose that $a,b,c$ are in both arithmetic progression and geometric progression. Prove that $a=b=c$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$a=b=c$.\n\nWriting $b=a+d$ and $c=a+2d$, the geometric progression condition gives $d^2=0$, so $d=0$.",
      "worked": "Since the terms are in arithmetic progression, write $b=a+d$ and $c=a+2d$. The geometric progression condition gives $b^2=ac$.\n$$\\begin{align*}(a+d)^2&=a(a+2d)\\\\a^2+2ad+d^2&=a^2+2ad\\\\d^2&=0\\\\d&=0.\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore, $b=a$ and $c=a$, so $a=b=c$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 40
  },
